ructure, and the control plane. Gateways tunnel traffic, cloud nodes run middleboxes, and the controller manages policies and tunnel mapping. Registration and addressing models are explained, including protected, DNS, and legacy services.\n\n5. Evaluation\nAPLOMB introduces negligible performance impact. Page load times, BitTorrent transfers, and VoIP jitter remain within acceptable limits. Dynamic scaling adapts to load within seconds. A case study on a large enterprise shows 60-90% of middleboxes can be outsourced. Latency penalties average ~1ms, and bandwidth costs can be reduced by 30% with APLOMB+.\n\n6. Discussion\nThe cloud trend supports APLOMB's hybrid model. Bandwidth pricing and security remain concerns. While encryption protects against external threats, trust in cloud providers is required. The model works well with modern enterprise migration trends to hybrid and cloud-first architectures.\n\n7. Related Work\nAPLOMB builds upon prior work in cloud computing, middlebox management, and redirection services, but is unique in proposing comprehensive cloud-based middlebox outsourcing. It differentiates from local solutions like ETTM and management-focused designs.\n\n8. Conclusion\nAPLOMB offers a viable approach to reduce costs and complexity in enterprise networking. It can outsource the majority of middlebox functions without significant performance trade-offs and is applicable to a range of enterprise sizes."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Got it!'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What distinguishes APLOMB from ETTM?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'APLOMB differentiates itself from ETTM (Enterprise Threat Management) in that it provides a comprehensive middlebox management approach, with a focus on security and proactive detection of threats. It offers a more integrated solution that automates the detection and management of security threats in the cloud environment. This approach is designed to handle a wider range of enterprise environments an